WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.320
There are two types of mindset we can
cultivate:

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.500
one that embraces problems as opportunities to learn

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:10.100
and one that avoids them often out of fear to fail.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.980
People that avoid conflicts can be
described as having a fixed mindset.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:19.440
Those who see problems as interesting
challenges have a growth mindset.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.700
Sometimes we like to switch from one to
the other.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:31.600
People have a fixed mindset because they believe the basic qualities like intelligence or talents are fixed traits

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.900
and that these traits are responsible for success.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:39.200
They often like to document past achievements.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:45.200
With a growth mindset people believe that new abilities can be developed through practice.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:51.080
This view creates a love for learning the most great leaders and artists have in common.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:57.960
For them life becomes an exciting journey with endless opportunity to figure out new things and advance.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:05.800
To develop a growth mindset Dr. Carol Dweck the Stanford University professor who coined the term,

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:10.100
advises leaders, teachers, and parents to celebrate trying.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:14.820
Teachers should applaud
students for any grade if they studied hard.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:19.700
Parents should encourage their children to develop any new skill they are interested in.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.220
Doing this will make them
learn the skill of learning

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.460
which will also help them back in the classroom.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:32.540
To illustrate the difference in everyday life let's observe two imaginary kids:

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.620
Jay thinks you've either got it or you haven't.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.700
Ann knows that she can learn
anything if she wants it enough.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:47.620
At physical exercise Jay avoid challenges
when it's time to jump over the vaulting horse

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:50.720
he's afraid to look stupid and being laughed at.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:54.800
Ann embraces any challenge. It's exciting and fun!

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:57.920
She knows that failing is part of learning

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:01.880
and if she tries hard, at the end nobody will laugh at her.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.620
Jay avoids feedback.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:09.220
If the teacher tells him how to improve an assignment he has been working on

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:11.060
he takes it personally.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:15.900
Ann knows that to improve she needs to listen to constructive criticism.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:19.700
She also understands that it's not her
that is being assessed

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:22.520
but the results of her work on that one day.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.620
Jay always takes the easy road.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:30.720
For example, he likes escalators and hates to take the stairs.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:35.580
When he is practicing the guitar he stops the moment he is getting stuck.

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:39.380
Ann usually doesn't even take escalators,

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:42.680
she jumps up the stairs counting every step in her head

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.900
and enjoys feeling the blood rushing through her veins.

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:49.520
She practices the drums every morning
for 15 minutes.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:51.320
Not that she always enjoys it,

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:56.340
but she knows that effort is
part of a journey to a more fun life.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.160
Ann likes to see others succeed,

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:00.940
it inspires her.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:06.760
She knows that if she motivates her friends to get better she herself is likely to grow too.

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:09.660
If his friends try new things and succeed

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:11.140
Jay feels threatened.

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:16.440
He's afraid that their success will put
pressure on him to do more with his life too.

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:21.480
Modern companies look for employees with the growth mindset

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:25.440
because they solve problems and persist despite obstacles.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:28.700
To spot the right ones, some asked during the interview

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:31.400
whether the job applicant believes if managers are born

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:34.040
or if management is a skill learned.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.660
Jay thinks
that managers are born.

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:38.740
Ann gets the job.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:42.940
Neuroscientist support the idea.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:48.360
They confirm that the brain grows like any other muscle in the body with training.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:52.680
Studies show that adopted twins tend to
have higher intelligence

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:56.560
Compared to their siblings who stayed with their biological parents.

00:03:57.020 --> 00:04:01.120
The difference appears to come from the higher educational levels of adoptive parents

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:05.160
and shows that nurture is more important
than nature.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:09.740
A simple switch in how a person views a situation

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:11.460
can mean the world of difference.

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:14.180
Not just the outcome of that situation,

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:17.140
the outcome of that person's place in life.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:20.560
As the late poet Samuel Beckett once said:

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:21.680
Ever tried.

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:23.100
Ever failed

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:24.240
No matter.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:25.560
Try again.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:26.660
Fail again.

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:28.510
Fail better.

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:31.980
What do you think about the concept?

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:33.660
Is it overly simplistic?

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:35.900
And if you buy the idea,

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:38.240
do you believe it is possible to make a
permanent switch

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:41.180
from a fixed to a growth mindset?

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:43.300
Please share your thoughts in the

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:45.600
comment section below!

